.TH MU2E-SSH-PROBE 1 "2026-09-17" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2e-ssh-probe \- show, or run, the exact ssh command the recovery tools use
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2e-ssh-probe
.RI [ HOST ...]
.RB [ \-c
.IR CLASS ]
.RB [ \-\-root ]
.RB [ \-\-run
.IR COMMAND ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
When a check reports that a node could not be reached, the next question is
always what command was actually issued, and through which gateway. This prints
that command verbatim so it can be pasted into a shell and debugged by hand,
and with
.B \-\-run
executes it and shows what came back.
.PP
It builds the command through the same code path the phases use \(em the same
jump-host selection, the same
.B ssh
options, the same root login \(em so what it prints is what a real run does.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I HOST
Nodes to probe, short or fully qualified. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-c " CLASS" "\fR, \fP" \-\-class " CLASS"
Probe every node of this host class.
.TP
.BI \-l " NAME" "\fR, \fP" \-\-location " NAME"
Location to resolve names in.
.TP
.B \-\-root
Use the root login
.RI ( ssh.root_user )
rather than the ordinary one.
.TP
.BI \-\-run " COMMAND"
Actually run this command and show the result. Without it, nothing is executed.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
Override the command timeout.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the argument vectors and results as JSON.
.SH EXAMPLES
What would be run, without running it:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ssh-probe mu2e-trk-03
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check a mount as root, through the gateway, exactly as the tools would:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ssh-probe mu2e-trk-03 \-\-root \-\-run 'mountpoint \-q /home; echo $?'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Confirm every tracker node answers at all:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ssh-probe \-c tracker \-\-run true
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every probe succeeded, or nothing was run.
.TP
.B 1
At least one node failed.
.TP
.B 2
No node was named.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2e-node-inventory (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5)
